# Abstract storage layer: design doc

## 1. Goal

Move blog folder storage (`.folder`) and generated-asset storage (`.assets`)
off EBS onto S3, for cost/scalability/deployment reasons, and enforce
per-blog storage quotas — with no added read-path latency, paying any cost
at sync/build time instead. Git-backed sync is out of scope; if it's
revisited later its data can live under `folder/{blogID}/gitClient/...`
without the storage layer needing to know git exists.

## 2. Current state

- `config/index.js:87-93` derives everything off `BLOT_DATA_DIRECTORY`:
  `blog_static_files_dir` (`/static`) and `blog_folder_dir` (`/blogs`). No
  `app/storage` module exists yet.
- [`app/helper/localPath.js`](app/helper/localPath.js) resolves
  `(blogID, relativePath)` to an absolute path but does no IO — every caller
  does its own raw `fs`/`fs-extra` call. ~60 files call `localPath`, including
  23 in sync clients (`app/clients/*`) and 14 more that read
  `blog_folder_dir` directly (e.g. `app/blog/routes/assets.js`,
  `app/cdn/index.js`, `app/models/template/*`, `app/models/blog/remove.js`,
  `app/dashboard/site/export.js`).
- `blog_static_files_dir` has 21 non-test call sites; ~16 of them build
  `path.join(config.blog_static_files_dir, blogID, ...)` inline with no
  shared helper (thumbnail creation, document converters, image
  optimization, link screenshots, dashboard uploads, blog removal/export,
  the CDN static route). Subdirectories in use: `_thumbnails`,
  `_image_cache`, `_assets`, `_bookmark_screenshots`, `_avatars`,
  `_template_assets` (see §3's table for which are regenerable).
- Both directories map to the public CDN URL as bare `{blogID}/{path}`
  today, no scope prefix — relevant to the object-key plan in §3.
- No exotic IO patterns — overwhelmingly `fs-extra` (`readFile`,
  `outputFile`, `remove`, `ensureDir`, `copy`) plus `express.static()` for
  serving. Refactor, not a rewrite.
- Rendered HTML/CSS/JS is Redis-cached by content hash
  (`app/blog/render/full-view-cache.js`, `app/cdn/index.js:70-113`), not
  read from disk per request.
- Generated assets (thumbnails, doc conversions, image optimization) are
  built asynchronously at sync/build time and written before any visitor
  requests them — the "pay the cost at build time" model already works for
  this half of storage.
- Two known uncached per-request disk reads: `lookupFile.js`'s
  case-insensitive lookups, and `app/blog/render/retrieve/folder.js`'s live
  `fs.readdir` + `fs.stat` per request. [PR #1896](https://github.com/davidmerfield/blot/pull/1896)
  (baking CDN links at build time) has merged and removes most of the
  `lookupFile.js` exposure; `retrieve/folder.js` is still live (§4c).
- The per-blog sync lock is **not** a local-disk lockfile — it's already a
  Redis mutex (`app/sync/lock.js`: token-checked `SET NX PX` with a
  heartbeat), so it already works across multiple app hosts. No migration
  needed there.
- No per-blog storage-size accounting or quota enforcement exists, except a
  manual audit script (`scripts/user/identify-overdue-users.js`, `du -sb`
  against both directories for disabled accounts).
- No AWS SDK dependency anywhere in the repo — greenfield integration.
- Don't store bulk file content in Redis (see reverted PR #1831); Redis is
  fine for small bounded metadata (locks, directory listings, cache
  entries).

## 3. Decisions

- **Location: `app/storage`**, not `app/models/storage`. `app/models` is for
  structured domain entities with identity; a blob store is bytes-in/out
  keyed by path. `app/storage` sits alongside `app/models`, `app/helper`,
  `app/clients`, `app/build` as its own top-level concern.
- **Interface: plain functions per scope** (`storage/assets`, later
  `storage/folder`) taking `blogID` first, e.g.
  `assets.path(blogID, "_thumbnails", name)`. No classes — matches the rest
  of `app/`. The API grows only as call sites need it.
- **Naming: a small custom vocabulary** — `read`/`write`/`delete`/`list`/
  `exists`/`size`, not `fs-extra`'s names. `fs-extra` supports operations
  that don't map onto S3 (symlinks, `chmod`, atomic rename); reusing its
  names would invite callers to assume that surface exists.
- **One bucket**, not two. `.folder` under `folder/{blogID}/{path}`;
  `.assets` at the bucket root as `{blogID}/{path}` so its keys are
  identical to its public CDN paths (blog IDs are always `blog_<uuid>`,
  never `folder`, so the two can't collide). Storage class is set per
  object at write time rather than via prefix-filtered lifecycle rules.
  Two buckets are only needed if versioning/cross-region replication is
  wanted later (e.g. for a "restore deleted file" feature).
- **Versioning: off**, bucket-wide.
- **Backup: hourly `aws s3 sync` (whole bucket, no `--delete`) to
  Backblaze B2**, US West region (closest to the app's `us-west-2`; B2's
  region is a permanent per-account choice). No-`--delete` plus B2's
  always-on versioning gives grow-and-expire semantics: a
  `NoncurrentVersionExpiration` lifecycle rule (retention window TBD, see
  §6) cleans up superseded versions without ever expiring unchanged current
  objects. Decided over a second AWS account or Bunny Storage, for genuine
  vendor independence (a second cloud problem shouldn't be able to take out
  both the primary and the backup). Projected ~80% reduction in combined
  storage + backup spend versus current EBS; figures deliberately kept out
  of this doc.
- **No S3 Express One Zone** — single-AZ durability is a bad trade for data
  that already has a reduced backup posture, and its latency benefit
  targets the same problem an SSD cache (if built, §5) would solve.
- **Single region.** Future multi-region compute reads across the network
  from one bucket; live multi-region storage replication would add
  cross-region conflict-resolution risk the current single-writer-per-blog
  lock isn't built to handle.
- **One IAM role**, not scoped per prefix — revisit only if a concrete need
  for narrower blast radius shows up.
- **Separate bucket per environment** (dev/staging/prod).
- **Dev/test: MinIO + toxiproxy** in the dev docker-compose stack, the same
  way Redis latency is already simulated.
- **Bunny unchanged.** The app server stays Bunny's origin in every planned
  phase — `app/cdn/index.js` and `app/blog/routes/assets.js` keep serving
  the same routes, just backed by S3 instead of local disk. After writing a
  new asset, issue one GET to its public CDN URL to warm Bunny's
  Perma-Cache (the only documented way to populate it — there's no
  supported direct-write path). Direct-to-S3 serving is a possible later
  step, not part of this plan.
- **`.assets` holds non-regenerable dashboard uploads**, not just
  generated files:

  | Subdir | Written by | Regenerable? |
  |---|---|---|
  | `_thumbnails` | `app/build/thumbnail/create.js` | Yes, from `.folder` |
  | `_image_cache` | `app/build/plugins/image/optimize.js` | Yes, from `.folder` |
  | `_assets` | document converters | Yes, from `.folder` |
  | `_bookmark_screenshots` | `app/build/plugins/linkScreenshot` | Not faithfully — source page may have changed |
  | `_avatars` | `app/dashboard/site/save/avatar.js` | No — exists only in Blot |
  | `_template_assets` | template editor uploads | No — exists only in Blot |

  Nothing may wipe the whole `.assets` scope except blog deletion (today
  `app/sync/rebuild.js` only `fs.emptyDir`s `_thumbnails`/`_image_cache`,
  and the storage layer shouldn't expose a scope-wide delete). Quota is
  enforced on `.folder` writes only; `.assets` bytes are tracked but not
  capped.
- **Bucket stays private for now.** The app is the origin in every planned
  phase, so there's no current need for public reads. Public-read
  `GetObject` (never `ListBucket`, never write) would be acceptable later
  if direct-to-S3 serving is wanted — blog folders are already served
  unauthenticated via `/folder/v-:version`, and blog IDs are UUIDs, so this
  wouldn't be a new exposure.

## 4. Caveats for moving `.folder` to S3

**a. mtime is used as data — the biggest risk.** Several places treat
filesystem mtime/ctime as meaningful, not incidental:
- Google Drive (`app/clients/google-drive/util/download.js:44,194,411-414`)
  and iCloud (`app/clients/icloud/sync/util/download.js:31`) set local
  mtimes with `fs.utimes` to mirror the remote provider; iCloud's upload
  route compares `stat.mtime` to a stored modified time for change
  detection (`app/clients/icloud/routes/site/upload.js:47`).
- `app/blog/render/retrieve/folder.js:71` exposes `stat.mtime` to templates
  as `updated`.
- `app/build/index.js:166` and `app/build/multiple.js:105-129` feed mtime
  into entry metadata.
- `app/build/plugins/folderAssets/index.js:317,329` and
  `app/blog/render/replaceFolderLinks/lookupFile.js:96` hash
  mtime+ctime+size into CDN version tokens.
- `app/dashboard/site/folder/stat.js` shows mtime to users.

S3 can't set arbitrary `Last-Modified`, and ctime doesn't exist there.
Implication: store mtime as object metadata (e.g. `x-amz-meta-mtime`) at
write time, have the storage layer's `stat()` return it, and migrate every
reader above to the storage layer's `stat()` before cutover. Version tokens
that currently hash mtime+ctime should move to a content hash / ETag
instead.

**b. Sync clients need a staging pattern, not direct S3 calls.** Google
Drive streams to a temp file then moves it into place; Dropbox's
`delta.js` does recursive `readdir` diffs; iCloud's `resolveCaseConflicts.js`
resolves case conflicts via rename. These all assume a real local
filesystem underneath and need a stage-locally-then-commit pattern against
the storage layer, not raw S3 calls threaded through each client.

**c. Request-time folder reads still exist, even after PR #1896.**
`app/blog/routes/assets.js` serves from the blog folder with case-folding
and index.html/.html fallbacks; `app/cdn/index.js`'s `/folder/v-:version`
route uses `express.static()` plus `assertNoSymlinks`; `lookupFile.js`
remains live for templates; `retrieve/folder.js` still does an uncached
`readdir`+`stat` per request. All of these need to move onto the storage
layer (with either a local cache or measured-acceptable S3 latency) before
`.folder` moves. Recommendation: measure real S3 latency/throughput first
(a sampled shadow-read alongside real reads during the dual-write phase)
before building a caching layer — much of what would hit S3 directly is
already narrowed by Redis-cached render output, Bunny Perma-Cache on
generated assets, and Origin Shield deduplicating concurrent misses. Build
an SSD-LRU cache (there's a working precedent for this pattern in
`app/clients/icloud/macserver/watcher/monitorDiskUsage.js`) only if the
measurement shows it's needed.

**d. `app/sync/rebuild.js:113-124` processes files serially**
(`async.eachSeries`) — a full rebuild of a large blog would turn into
thousands of serial S3 round trips. Needs bounded concurrency (e.g.
`async.eachLimit`) before `.folder` goes to S3.

**e. ~66 `err.code === "ENOENT"` checks exist in `app/`.** The storage
layer needs one backend-agnostic not-found signal (e.g. a `NotFoundError`
class) that means the same thing on local disk (`ENOENT`) and S3
(`404`/`NoSuchKey`).

**f. Symlink checks become moot on S3** — `assertNoSymlinks` has nothing to
check once the backend has no filesystem.

**g. Case sensitivity.** S3 keys are case-sensitive, which removes a
filesystem quirk rather than adding one. Case-insensitive resolution
(today duplicated between `lookupFile.js` and `helper/caseSensitivePath.js`)
should consolidate into one storage-layer method, exposed explicitly
(`store.folder.resolveCaseInsensitive(path)`) rather than built into
`.read()`'s hot path — it's specifically a user-authored-link problem
(`![](image.JPG)` vs. `image.jpg`), not a general storage concern, and
`.assets` paths are always Blot-generated so it doesn't apply there.

## 5. Phases

- **Phase 0 (in progress):** `app/storage/assets.js` centralizes
  generated-asset paths — `path(blogID, ...segments)` and
  `removeAll(blogID)` — over today's local disk, no behavior change. Migrate the 21 `blog_static_files_dir` call
  sites onto it. Note: 7 of those sites hand a path to a tool that writes
  itself (sharp's `.toFile`, pandoc `--extract-media`, puppeteer
  screenshots, thumbnail/favicon generators), so the API shape is
  "write locally, then commit," not `write(buffer)`. `removeAll(blogID)`
  is the only whole-scope delete, used solely by blog deletion.
- **Phase 1 — `.assets` first.** These files are written asynchronously,
  content-addressed or UUID-keyed, and never change once written — the
  simplest shape to move. Add a `commit()` hook at write sites: dual-write
  local + S3, read-through with local as cache. The B2 backup must already
  be running before local disk stops holding a copy — dashboard uploads in
  this scope can't be regenerated. Warm Bunny's Perma-Cache with a GET in
  the same async step. Add sampled shadow-read timing instrumentation here
  to start gathering real S3-vs-local numbers.
- **Phase 2 — `.folder`.** Grow the storage-layer API as call sites need it
  (read/write/remove/list/stat, with mtime as metadata per §4a), migrate
  sync clients onto a staging pattern, resolve the caveats in §4, parallelize
  `rebuild.js`, then cut over. Add a paginated `list()` when a caller
  actually needs pagination — `retrieve/folder.js` is the motivating case.
  Build an SSD cache (Phase 2b) only if the Phase 1 measurements show pure
  S3 latency is a real problem; if built, invalidate per-object using the
  existing sync-completion pub/sub (`app/sync/index.js`'s
  `blog:{blogID}:preview:reload` broadcast), extended to carry which paths
  changed.
- **Quotas** — decouple from the storage-layer build-out. Maintain a
  per-blog byte counter in `app/sync/update` (which already sees every
  changed path), backed by periodic `du`/`ListObjects` reconciliation to
  catch drift. Can ship independently of, and before, the S3 work.

## 6. Directory listing

When `.folder` gets a listing primitive (Phase 2), make it a paginated
`.list()` from the start — an unpaginated `readdir()`-equivalent would only
mean migrating every call site off it again later.

```js
const { entries, cursor } = await store.folder.list(path, { cursor, limit });
const allEntries = await store.folder.listAll(path); // buffers everything; for callers that can afford it
```

S3's `ListObjectsV2` is natively paginated (1,000 keys/call,
`NextContinuationToken`); `.list()`'s cursor can just be that token. On
local disk, `fs.promises.opendir()` gives an unbuffered async-iterable
directory handle — implementing `.list()` on top of it starts fixing
`retrieve/folder.js`'s existing memory-crash risk immediately, independent
of S3. `.listAll()` is for callers like `buildFromFolder`'s directory scan
or the quota-reconciliation script that genuinely need everything; the
`folder` template local — the one place a huge directory has actually
caused problems — should call `.list()` directly, never `.listAll()`.

One open tradeoff: `opendir`'s streaming iteration doesn't guarantee a
stable sort order, and a meaningful opaque cursor generally wants one. If
the `folder` template local needs deterministic ordering, the local backend
may need to do more than pure unbuffered streaming — not yet decided.

## 7. Open questions

- B2 `NoncurrentVersionExpiration` retention window (60? 90 days?).
- Shadow-read instrumentation sample rate for Phase 1 (1%? 5%?).
- `.list()`'s ordering guarantee (§6).
- Whether an SSD cache (Phase 2b) ends up being needed at all — resolvable
  by the Phase 1 shadow-read measurements rather than by judgment call.
- A one-time scan of existing filenames for anything that's safe on POSIX
  but odd as a raw S3 key (leading/trailing whitespace, control
  characters), before `.folder` migrates.
